---
import BlogArticle from "../../components/BlogArticle.astro";
---

<BlogArticle
	title="PAN Card Signature Size in Pixels and KB: NSDL vs UTIITSL"
	description="Compare commonly quoted NSDL/Protean and UTIITSL PAN signature size presets in pixels, centimetres, DPI and KB, with current-form guidance."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/pan-card-signature-size/"
	heading="PAN card signature size in pixels and KB: NSDL vs UTIITSL"
	intro="There is no one signature size for every PAN application. The correct dimensions and file limit depend on the application route and the current instructions shown by that provider."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Commonly quoted legacy signature presets</h2>
		<div class="overflow-x-auto">
			<table class="w-full min-w-xl border-collapse text-left text-sm">
				<thead class="bg-surface-soft text-ink">
					<tr>
						<th class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">Legacy route preset</th>
						<th class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">Common dimensions</th>
						<th class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">Common file limit</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">Protean / NSDL reference</td>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">4.5 × 2 cm; about 354 × 157 px at 200 DPI</td>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">50 KB</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">UTIITSL reference</td>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">400 × 200 px at 600 DPI</td>
						<td class="border border-hairline px-4 py-3">60 KB</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			These are widely repeated older specifications, not a promise
			about the current upload screen. Since April 2026, new Indian PAN
			applications use Form 93 or Form 94. Check that form’s current
			requirements before using a legacy NSDL PAN card photo resizer or
			UTI PAN card photo resizer preset.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">PAN card signature size in KB, pixels and DPI</h2>
		<p>
			Pixels set the image’s width and height; they do not determine
			its file size by themselves. A noisy or highly detailed signature
			scan can be larger than a clean, tightly cropped version at the
			same pixel dimensions. DPI is chiefly relevant when a form
			specifies a physical size or print resolution; it is not a
			substitute for setting the right pixel dimensions.
		</p>
		<p>
			For the common legacy Protean size, converting 4.5 × 2 cm at 200
			DPI gives roughly 354 × 157 pixels after rounding. Keep the
			original aspect ratio and do not stretch the handwritten mark to
			fill the entire rectangle.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Prepare and verify the signature file</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Sign with the writing instrument and on the background requested by the current application. A common preparation method is dark ink on plain white paper.</li>
			<li>Scan or photograph the original evenly, then crop to include the full signature and a small margin.</li>
			<li>Use a <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card signature resizer</a> to set the current requested dimensions, then export to the accepted format.</li>
			<li>Check the saved file’s extension, dimensions and actual size in the file information on your device. Reopen it at full size to check readability.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Do not copy, redraw, or alter the signature to make it look like
			a different signature. Compression should reduce file weight,
			not remove strokes or change the appearance.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		For the latest instructions, use
		<a class="text-link" href="https://tinpan.proteantech.in/services/pan/instructions-form_93-form_94.html">Protean’s Form 93/94 instructions</a>
		or the <a class="text-link" href="https://www.pan.utiitsl.com/PAN/newA.html">UTIITSL Form 93/94 page</a>.
		This independent resizer is not affiliated with NSDL, Protean or UTIITSL.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
